{
  "dom_id": "step-q104973",
  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14101062/assets/94c9daf5f16776eef520e40f68a38dc5e05c4ceb5470cdc18880ac288a9ce031.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:[-1,5]\\to [-1,4]$ whose graph is shown above?\n\n- The range of $f$ is $[-1,3]$\n- $\u2200\\,y\\in [-1,3],$ $\u2203\\,x\\in [-1,5]$ such that $f(x)=y$\n- $f$ is a surjection",
  "worked_solution": "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection (also known as *onto*) if, for *every* element $y$ in $Y,$ there is *at least one* element in $X$ that maps to $y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $\u2200\\,y\\in Y,$ $\u2203\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, the highest $y$-value is $3$ and the lowest $y$-value is $-1.$ The graph covers all $y$-values between $-1$ and $3$, including these numbers, so the range of the function is $[-1,3].$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since the range of $f$ is $[-1,3]$ and $[-1,3]$ is contained in that range, it follows that for all $y$ contained within $[-1,3],$ there exists some $x$ contained within $[-1,5]$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. As we can see from the range, there is no real number $x$ such that $f(x)=4.$ So, it's *not true* that for all $y$ contained within $[-1,4],$ there exists some $x$ contained within $[-1,5]$ such that $f(x)=y.$ In conclusion, $f$ is not a surjection of $[-1,5]$ onto $[-1,4].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
